# macOS-specific ffmpeg flags. Layered on top of common/ffmpeg-features.

--enable-cross-compile

# Apple's own hardware paths: VideoToolbox covers h264/hevc/prores/av1 decode
# and encode on both Intel and Apple Silicon, so there is no NVIDIA/AMD/Vulkan
# equivalent to enable here -- ffnvcodec and amf are x86/Windows-and-Linux only,
# and Vulkan on macOS would mean shipping MoltenVK, a real runtime dependency.
#
# Consequence worth knowing: ffmpeg only probes for a SPIR-V compiler inside
# `if enabled vulkan`, so with vulkan off macOS gets no spirv_compiler and none
# of the 18 Vulkan compute filters or the ProRes/FFV1/DPX/APV Vulkan hwaccels
# that Linux and Windows get. That is also why macOS/deps.sh does not install
# glslang.
--enable-avfoundation
--enable-indev=avfoundation
--enable-videotoolbox
# GPU filters on macOS, where there is no Vulkan (see above): scale_vt runs on
# VideoToolbox frames (already in), and the `coreimage` / `coreimagesrc`
# filters expose every Core Image filter (blur, colour, distortion, generators)
# on the GPU. yadif_videotoolbox additionally needs --enable-metal, which
# macOS/ffmpeg.sh adds when the Metal compiler is present on the runner.
--enable-coreimage
# coreimage_filter_deps="coreimage appkit", and appkit is in configure's
# autodetect list, i.e. off under --disable-autodetect unless asked for.
--enable-appkit
--enable-audiotoolbox

# openssl (built by macOS/openssl.sh into $INSTALL_PREFIX/openssl), NOT
# SecureTransport. SecureTransport is deprecated by Apple and, more concretely,
# ffmpeg's dtls_protocol_deps_any is "openssl schannel gnutls mbedtls" -- it
# does not include securetransport, so the whip muxer and srtp would be
# unavailable on macOS without this. libsrt links the same openssl.
--enable-openssl
--disable-securetransport

# zlib and bzlib come from the shared file and resolve against the macOS SDK's
# own libz/libbz2 -- OS libraries, so no leak and nothing to ship. lzma is the
# exception: the SDK has liblzma.tbd but NO lzma.h (checked on macOS 15 and 26),
# so ffmpeg's `check_lib lzma lzma.h ...` cannot pass and, since the flag would
# be an explicit request, configure would die. Linux and Windows get it from
# their sysroot's xz.
--disable-lzma

--disable-libxcb
